var e=[{topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A prescription is a written order from a registered medical practitioner to a pharmacist, directing that a stated medicine be supplied to a named patient with stated directions for use. Reading one correctly is the first professional skill a dispensing pharmacist learns.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Parts of a prescription, in order`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Date of writing the prescription.`,`Name, age, sex and address of the patient. Age is essential for children and the elderly so the dose can be checked.`,`Superscription: the symbol Rx, from the Latin recipe, meaning take thou. It heads the order.`,`Inscription: the body of the prescription, giving the name and quantity of each ingredient.`,`Subscription: the directions to the pharmacist, for example make a mixture, send twenty tablets.`,`Signatura (Signa or Sig): the directions for the patient, which the pharmacist transfers to the label.`,`Renewal or refill instruction, where allowed.`,`Signature, registration number and address of the prescriber.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Parts of the inscription`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Base or basis: the active medicament that produces the intended effect.`,`Adjuvant: an ingredient that assists or increases the action of the base.`,`Corrective (corrigent): masks or corrects an unpleasant taste, odour or an unwanted effect.`,`Vehicle: the liquid or solid carrier that gives the preparation its bulk and makes it easy to take.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Latin abbreviations survive on prescriptions because they are short and internationally understood. You must be able to translate them on sight, since the whole label depends on them.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Subscription and signatura are different`,text:`The subscription talks to the pharmacist (how to make and how much to send). The signatura talks to the patient (how to take it). Swapping the two is the most common mistake in this topic.`}]},{topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The working level is the abbreviation table, the sequence of handling a prescription in the dispensary, and the information that must legally appear on the label.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Latin abbreviations of frequency and timing`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Abbreviation`,`Latin`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`b.d. (b.i.d.)`,`bis in die`,`Twice a day`],[`t.d.s. (t.i.d.)`,`ter die sumendum`,`Three times a day`],[`q.i.d. (q.d.s.)`,`quater in die`,`Four times a day`],[`o.d.`,`omni die`,`Every day, once daily`],[`o.m.`,`omni mane`,`Every morning`],[`o.n.`,`omni nocte`,`Every night`],[`h.s.`,`hora somni`,`At bedtime`],[`stat`,`statim`,`Immediately, at once`],[`p.r.n.`,`pro re nata`,`When required`],[`ad lib.`,`ad libitum`,`As much as desired, freely`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Abbreviations of quantity, route and compounding`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Abbreviation`,`Latin`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`a.c.`,`ante cibum`,`Before food`],[`p.c.`,`post cibum`,`After food`],[`q.s.`,`quantum sufficit`,`A sufficient quantity`],[`aa`,`ana`,`Of each (equal quantity of each)`],[`ad`,`ad`,`Up to, to make up to`],[`gtt`,`gutta / guttae`,`Drop or drops`],[`m.d.u.`,`more dicto utendus`,`To be used as directed`],[`p.o.`,`per os`,`By mouth`],[`ft.`,`fiat`,`Let it be made`],[`aq.`,`aqua`,`Water`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Dosage form abbreviations often seen: mist. (mistura, mixture), pulv. (pulvis, powder), ung. (unguentum, ointment), lot. (lotio, lotion), garg. (gargarisma, gargle), collut. (collutorium, mouthwash), collyr. (collyrium, eye lotion), lin. (linimentum, liniment), supp. (suppository), syr. (syrupus, syrup).`,`div. in p. aeq. means divide into equal parts, used when a powder is to be wrapped as divided doses.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Handling a prescription`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Receive the prescription and read it completely before touching any container.`,`Check that it is complete and legal: patient details, date, drug, dose, quantity, signature and registration number of the prescriber.`,`Check the dose against the normal range for that age and weight, and look for incompatibilities and interactions.`,`Collect the ingredients, checking each label three times: on picking up, on measuring and on returning to the shelf.`,`Compound or count the medicine accurately, then check the finished product.`,`Label and pack it appropriately.`,`Number the prescription and enter it in the prescription register where the law requires a record.`,`Price it, hand it over and counsel the patient on how to take it and how to store it.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Labelling`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The label must carry the patient's name, the name of the preparation or its ingredients, the directions for use in plain language, the date of dispensing, and the name and address of the pharmacy.`,`Auxiliary or cautionary labels are added as needed: shake well before use, for external use only, not to be taken, store in a cool place, keep out of the reach of children.`,`By convention a white label is used for preparations to be taken internally and a red or otherwise distinctive label for preparations for external use.`,`Directions must be written in the language the patient understands, never left in Latin.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`o.d. is ambiguous`,text:`In older ophthalmic usage o.d. meant oculus dexter, the right eye, while in general usage it means omni die, once daily. Because of this ambiguity such abbreviations are discouraged in modern safe-prescribing practice and the words should be written out.`}]},{topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work covers the legal checks a pharmacist must make before dispensing, what to do with a doubtful prescription, and the records that must be kept.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Legal checks before dispensing`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Drugs in Schedule H, Schedule H1 and Schedule X of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules may be sold only against the prescription of a registered medical practitioner.`,`Schedule H1 was introduced to control certain antibiotics, anti-tuberculosis drugs and habit-forming drugs; the supply must be entered in a separate register giving the prescriber and patient details, and that register is retained for the period specified in the Rules.`,`Schedule X drugs, the psychotropic substances listed there, require a prescription written in duplicate, one copy of which the pharmacist retains, and they must be kept under lock and key.`,`Narcotic drugs are additionally governed by the NDPS Act and its rules.`,`The dispensing must be done by or under the direct supervision of a registered pharmacist, as required by the Pharmacy Act.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Doubtful and illegible prescriptions`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Never guess a drug name or a dose. Similar-looking names are a recognised cause of dispensing error.`,`If the dose lies outside the usual range, or an interaction or incompatibility is suspected, contact the prescriber and record the conversation.`,`A pharmacist may not change the drug, the strength or the dose on their own initiative; the change must come from the prescriber.`,`In a genuine emergency, refusing to dispense may itself cause harm, so the correct step is to seek confirmation quickly rather than simply to send the patient away.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Error type`,`Example`,`Preventive step`],rows:[[`Look-alike name`,`Confusing two similar drug names`,`Read the whole prescription, query the prescriber`],[`Decimal point error`,`A trailing zero read as ten-fold dose`,`Never use a trailing zero; use a leading zero`],[`Wrong strength`,`Two strengths of the same brand`,`Three-point label check`],[`Wrong patient`,`Two patients of the same surname`,`Check name, age and address on hand-over`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Abbreviations such as U for units, and the use of a trailing zero after a decimal point, are recognised sources of ten-fold overdose and are avoided in modern practice.`,`Repeat or refill supplies are not permitted for prescription drugs where the rules require a fresh prescription for each supply.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The three-point check`,text:`Read the container label when you take it from the shelf, when you pour or count, and when you replace it. This simple discipline is asked about directly and is the standard answer to how dispensing errors are prevented.`}]},{topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Posology is the branch of pharmacy and medicine that deals with doses. A dose is the quantity of a drug given at one time to produce the desired therapeutic effect, and the pharmacist must be able to check whether the dose written on a prescription is reasonable for that patient.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Kinds of dose`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Minimum effective dose: the smallest amount that produces a therapeutic effect.`,`Therapeutic (usual) dose: the amount normally given to produce the desired effect safely.`,`Maximum dose: the largest amount that can be given without producing toxicity.`,`Toxic dose: the amount that produces harmful effects.`,`Lethal dose: the amount that causes death.`,`Loading (priming) dose: a large first dose given to reach the effective concentration quickly.`,`Maintenance dose: the repeated dose that keeps the concentration in the therapeutic range.`,`Divided dose: the total daily amount split into two or more portions during the day.`,`Prophylactic dose: given to prevent a disease rather than to treat it.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Factors that affect the dose`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Age: children and the elderly usually need less than the standard adult dose.`,`Body weight and body surface area: the standard adult dose assumes an adult of about 70 kg.`,`Sex and physiological state: pregnancy, lactation and menstruation may require caution or a change of drug.`,`Route of administration: an intravenous dose is smaller than an oral dose of the same drug because it avoids first pass metabolism and incomplete absorption.`,`Time of administration and presence of food: food slows or reduces absorption of many drugs.`,`Disease state: impaired kidney or liver function reduces elimination, so the dose must be reduced.`,`Genetic factors and idiosyncrasy, for example slow acetylators of isoniazid.`,`Tolerance, addiction and accumulation on repeated dosing.`,`Drug interactions, environmental factors and the emotional state of the patient.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Therapeutic index is not a dose`,text:`The therapeutic index is a ratio, usually LD50 divided by ED50, describing the margin of safety of a drug. It is not a quantity to be given, and a drug with a narrow therapeutic index simply needs closer dose control.`}]},{topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Children are not small adults, so several empirical rules exist to scale an adult dose down. Each rule uses a different variable, and GPAT questions usually give the variable and ask you to substitute.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Paediatric dose rules`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Young's rule: child dose = adult dose x age (years) / (age + 12)`,caption:`Uses age. Traditionally applied to children between 1 and 12 years.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Clark's rule: child dose = adult dose x weight in pounds / 150`,caption:`Uses body weight; 150 pounds is the assumed adult weight. In metric form, child dose = adult dose x weight in kg / 70.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Dilling's rule: child dose = adult dose x age (years) / 20`,caption:`Uses age; a simple linear rule, generally applied to older children.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Fried's rule: infant dose = adult dose x age in months / 150`,caption:`Uses age in months and is intended for infants under about two years.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Body surface area rule: child dose = adult dose x BSA of child (m2) / 1.73`,caption:`1.73 m2 is the accepted average body surface area of an adult. This is the most reliable of the rules.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Rule`,`Variable used`,`Best suited to`],rows:[[`Young's`,`Age in years`,`Children 1 to 12 years`],[`Clark's`,`Body weight`,`Any child, better than age rules`],[`Dilling's`,`Age in years`,`Older children`],[`Fried's`,`Age in months`,`Infants`],[`Body surface area`,`BSA in m2`,`Most accurate, used for cytotoxics`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Worked examples`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Young's rule: adult dose 500 mg, child aged 8 years. Dose = 500 x 8/(8 + 12) = 500 x 8/20 = 500 x 0.4 = 200 mg.`,`Clark's rule: adult dose 300 mg, child weighing 30 pounds. Dose = 300 x 30/150 = 300 x 0.2 = 60 mg.`,`Dilling's rule: adult dose 400 mg, child aged 10 years. Dose = 400 x 10/20 = 200 mg.`,`Fried's rule: adult dose 240 mg, infant aged 15 months. Dose = 240 x 15/150 = 24 mg.`,`Body surface area rule: adult dose 1000 mg, child of BSA 0.8 m2. Dose = 1000 x 0.8/1.73 = 462 mg.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which rule uses what`,text:`Clark and Weight both have a hard C-K sound of counting kilograms; Fried is for the tiny fry, so it uses months; Young and Dilling both use years, and Young alone adds 12 to the age.`}]},{topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced posology means calculating body surface area, adjusting for impaired renal function, and knowing why the elderly need smaller doses.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`BSA (m2) = square root of [height (cm) x weight (kg) / 3600]`,caption:`Mosteller formula for body surface area, the form most used in practice because it can be done on a simple calculator.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Worked example: a child 100 cm tall weighing 25 kg has BSA = square root of (100 x 25 / 3600) = square root of 0.6944 = 0.833 m2. If the adult dose is 600 mg, the child dose = 600 x 0.833 / 1.73 = 289 mg, say 290 mg.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Geriatric dosing`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Renal function falls steadily with age, so renally cleared drugs such as digoxin, aminoglycosides and lithium accumulate.`,`Hepatic blood flow and enzyme activity fall, reducing first pass metabolism and clearance.`,`Lean body mass and total body water fall while body fat rises, so water-soluble drugs reach higher concentrations and lipid-soluble drugs have a longer half-life.`,`Serum albumin falls, raising the free fraction of highly bound acidic drugs such as warfarin and phenytoin.`,`Homeostatic reflexes are blunted, so postural hypotension and sedation are more likely.`,`The practical rule is start low and go slow: begin at a reduced dose, titrate against response, and review the whole medication list for polypharmacy.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Renal dose adjustment`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`CrCl (mL/min) = (140 - age) x weight (kg) / (72 x serum creatinine in mg/dL)`,caption:`Cockcroft and Gault equation. Multiply the result by 0.85 for a female patient.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Worked example: a man of 60 years weighing 72 kg with serum creatinine 1.2 mg/dL has CrCl = (140 - 60) x 72 / (72 x 1.2) = 5760 / 86.4 = 66.7 mL/min.`,`Once clearance is known, the dose is adjusted either by reducing the size of each dose and keeping the interval, which gives smoother concentrations, or by keeping the dose and lengthening the interval, which preserves a high peak.`,`For a concentration-dependent antibiotic such as an aminoglycoside the interval is lengthened, because the high peak is what kills the organism and the trough must be allowed to fall.`,`For a time-dependent antibiotic such as a beta-lactam the dose size is reduced instead, so that the concentration stays above the minimum inhibitory concentration for longer.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Body surface area beats age and weight`,text:`When a question asks which method of calculating a paediatric dose is the most accurate, the answer is the body surface area method, because surface area tracks metabolic rate, cardiac output and renal function better than either age or weight alone.`}]},{topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`An incompatibility is an undesirable change that occurs when two or more substances in a prescription are brought together. The change may be in appearance, in chemical identity or in therapeutic effect, and the pharmacist must recognise it before the medicine leaves the counter.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three classes`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Physical incompatibility: no new chemical substance is formed, but the preparation is unacceptable in appearance or cannot be made at all. Also called pharmaceutical incompatibility.`,`Chemical incompatibility: the ingredients react and a new substance appears, seen as a precipitate, a gas, a colour change, or simply as loss of potency.`,`Therapeutic incompatibility: the preparation is pharmaceutically perfect, but the combination is wrong for the patient because of an interaction, a contraindication or a wrong dose.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Physical incompatibility, with examples`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Problem`,`Example`,`Correction`],rows:[[`Immiscibility`,`Fixed oil and water in one bottle`,`Add a suitable emulsifying agent`],[`Insolubility`,`Sulphur or a resin in water`,`Suspend with tragacanth or a wetting agent`],[`Liquefaction`,`Camphor with menthol or thymol`,`Add an inert absorbent, or dispense separately`],[`Precipitation`,`A resinous tincture added to water`,`Add slowly with stirring; use a dispersing agent`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`A liquefaction of this kind is called a eutectic mixture: two solids that each melt well above room temperature give a mixture whose melting point is below room temperature, so the powder turns damp or liquid in the mortar.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The classic eutectic pairs`,text:`Camphor, menthol, thymol, phenol, salol, betanaphthol, chloral hydrate, acetanilide and antipyrine are the substances repeatedly quoted as forming eutectic mixtures when triturated together. Any two of them in a powder prescription should ring an alarm.`}]},{topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The working level is chemical incompatibility, which is the largest group, and the professional rules for correcting each kind of incompatibility without altering the prescriber intention.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chemical incompatibility`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reaction`,`Example`,`What is seen`,`Correction`],rows:[[`Effervescence`,`Sodium bicarbonate with citric acid in water`,`Carbon dioxide gas`,`Dispense the parts separately, mix when used`],[`Precipitation`,`Alkaloidal salt with an alkali`,`Free alkaloid precipitates`,`Avoid the alkali or dispense as a suspension`],[`Precipitation`,`Silver nitrate with a soluble chloride`,`White silver chloride`,`Keep the two apart; supply separately`],[`Precipitation`,`Tannin-containing extract with an alkaloid`,`Insoluble alkaloidal tannate`,`Order of mixing, dilution, or separate supply`],[`Oxidation`,`Potassium permanganate with glycerin`,`Violent reaction, may ignite`,`Never triturate together; dispense separately`],[`Complexation`,`Tetracycline with a calcium or iron salt`,`Insoluble chelate, loss of absorption`,`Separate the times of administration`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Salting out is a further chemical effect: adding a large amount of an electrolyte to a solution of a gum, a soap or a volatile oil throws the dispersed substance out of solution.`,`Some chemical changes are deliberate and are not incompatibilities at all, for example the reaction between a bicarbonate and an acid in an effervescent granule, which is the intended action.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Tolerated and adjusted incompatibility`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tolerated incompatibility: the reaction can be prevented or made harmless by a change in technique, such as diluting the solutions, changing the order of mixing, or adding the reactive ingredient last. No ingredient is changed.`,`Adjusted incompatibility: the reaction can only be avoided by adding or replacing an ingredient, for example adding a suspending agent, or substituting a therapeutically equivalent salt. Any such change needs the agreement of the prescriber.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Therapeutic incompatibility`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Combination`,`Nature of the problem`],rows:[[`A sulphonamide with procaine`,`Procaine yields PABA, which antagonises the sulphonamide`],[`Tetracycline with an antacid or milk`,`Chelation with calcium, magnesium or aluminium blocks absorption`],[`Aspirin with warfarin`,`Additive bleeding risk, plus displacement from albumin`],[`Chloramphenicol with phenytoin`,`Metabolism of phenytoin is inhibited, causing toxicity`],[`An oral contraceptive with rifampicin`,`Enzyme induction lowers contraceptive efficacy`],[`Adrenaline with a non-selective beta blocker`,`Unopposed alpha action, hypertension`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Therapeutic incompatibility also covers a wrong dose, a drug contraindicated in that patient, and two drugs of the same class prescribed together by oversight.`,`The correction is never made by the pharmacist alone. The prescriber is contacted, the problem explained, and the change recorded.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`A perfectly clear mixture can still be incompatible`,text:`Therapeutic incompatibility produces no visible change at all. Questions that describe a preparation as elegant and stable but harmful to the patient are pointing at therapeutic, not physical or chemical, incompatibility.`}]},{topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work is about handling eutectics correctly and about the systematic decision the pharmacist makes when an incompatibility is found.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Eutectic mixtures in detail`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A eutectic mixture is the particular proportion of two solids that has the lowest melting point of any mixture of those two solids. If that eutectic temperature is below room temperature, the mixture liquefies when the components are triturated together.`,`The liquefaction is a physical change only; the substances are unchanged and can still be dispensed.`,`Modern use of this behaviour is deliberate, as in the eutectic mixture of lidocaine and prilocaine used as a topical anaesthetic cream.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`First choice: mix the components lightly, without pressure, on a paper or with a light touch in the mortar, so that the eutectic never forms.`,`Second choice: add an inert absorbent such as light magnesium oxide, light kaolin, starch or magnesium carbonate, and dispense as a dusting or divided powder.`,`Third choice: allow the eutectic to form deliberately, then absorb the liquid onto the absorbent so the mass is uniform.`,`Alternative: wrap and dispense the two components separately, with directions to mix at the time of use.`,`If the prescription is for an ointment or a liniment, the liquefaction is often no problem at all, because the product is meant to be a liquid or a semisolid.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`How the pharmacist decides`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Identify what kind of incompatibility it is: physical, chemical or therapeutic.`,`Decide whether the change harms the patient, the potency or only the appearance.`,`Try a change in technique first, since a tolerated incompatibility needs no consultation.`,`If an ingredient must be added or replaced, contact the prescriber, since that is an adjusted incompatibility.`,`Record what was done, and label the product with any special direction such as shake well before use.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`New substance formed?`,`Usually visible?`,`Who may correct it`],rows:[[`Physical`,`No`,`Yes`,`Pharmacist, by technique`],[`Chemical`,`Yes`,`Often, but not always`,`Pharmacist if tolerated, else prescriber`],[`Therapeutic`,`Usually no`,`No`,`Prescriber only`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Absorbents for eutectics`,text:`Light magnesium oxide, light magnesium carbonate, light kaolin and starch are the absorbents named again and again for eutectic powders. Note the word light: the light grades have a far larger bulk and surface area and take up the liquid better.`}]},{topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Extemporaneous dispensing is the preparation of a medicine in the pharmacy, for one named patient, at the time the prescription is presented. It rests on being able to express a strength as a percentage and on a few compounding habits that never change.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Expressing strength`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Per cent weight in volume (w/v): grams of solute in 100 mL of the finished preparation. Used for a solid dissolved or suspended in a liquid, for example 0.9 percent w/v sodium chloride injection.`,`Per cent weight in weight (w/w): grams of solute in 100 g of the finished preparation. Used for a solid in a solid or a semisolid, for example 2 percent w/w salicylic acid ointment.`,`Per cent volume in volume (v/v): millilitres of solute in 100 mL of the finished preparation. Used for a liquid in a liquid, for example 70 percent v/v alcohol.`,`Ratio strength: written as 1 in x, meaning one part of the substance in x parts of the finished product. Thus 1 in 1000 adrenaline solution is 0.1 percent w/v.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Quantity of solute (g) = percentage w/v x volume (mL) / 100`,caption:`The single equation behind almost every dispensing calculation involving a solid in a liquid.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Example: to make 500 mL of a 0.9 percent w/v sodium chloride solution you need 0.9 x 500 / 100 = 4.5 g of sodium chloride.`,`Example: a 1 in 5000 potassium permanganate solution is 100/5000 = 0.02 percent w/v, that is 0.02 g in 100 mL.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three extemporaneous forms you must know`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Mixture: a liquid preparation for internal use, containing one or more medicaments dissolved or suspended in a suitable vehicle, dispensed in a multidose bottle.`,`Lotion: a liquid preparation for external application to the skin without friction, which may be a solution, a suspension or an emulsion.`,`Powder: a solid dosage form of one or more finely divided medicaments, for internal use as a bulk or divided powder, or for external use as a dusting powder.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`w/v is not the same as w/w`,text:`One hundred millilitres of a solution does not weigh 100 g unless its density is exactly 1 g/mL. For anything other than dilute aqueous solutions you must use the density before converting between w/v and w/w.`}]},{topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Alligation is the arithmetic shortcut for mixing two strengths to get a third. There are two forms: alligation medial, which finds the strength of a mixture already made, and alligation alternate, which finds the proportions needed to reach a wanted strength.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Alligation medial: final strength = sum of (quantity x strength) / total quantity`,caption:`A simple weighted average, used when you already know how much of each strength was used.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Worked example: mixing 200 mL of 20 percent alcohol with 300 mL of 50 percent alcohol gives (200 x 20 + 300 x 50) / 500 = (4000 + 15000) / 500 = 19000 / 500 = 38 percent v/v.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Alligation alternate`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Write the strength of the strong preparation above the strength of the weak one, with the wanted strength in the middle.`,`Subtract diagonally: the difference between the wanted strength and the weak strength is the number of parts of the strong preparation.`,`The difference between the strong strength and the wanted strength is the number of parts of the weak preparation.`,`Convert the parts into actual quantities using the total volume required.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Worked example: to prepare 900 mL of 70 percent v/v alcohol from 95 percent and 50 percent alcohols. Parts of 95 percent = 70 - 50 = 20. Parts of 50 percent = 95 - 70 = 25. Total 45 parts. Volume of 95 percent = 20/45 x 900 = 400 mL, volume of 50 percent = 25/45 x 900 = 500 mL.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Worked example with water: to dilute 90 percent alcohol to 30 percent, treat water as zero percent. Parts of 90 percent = 30 - 0 = 30; parts of water = 90 - 30 = 60. So one volume of the strong alcohol is diluted with two volumes of water.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Proof spirit`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Proof spirit is a legally defined strength of ethanol used for excise purposes. It is that mixture of ethanol and water which at 51 degrees Fahrenheit weighs exactly twelve thirteenths of the weight of an equal volume of distilled water.`,`Proof spirit corresponds to about 57.1 percent v/v of ethanol, which is about 49.24 percent w/w.`,`A spirit stronger than proof spirit is described as over proof (O.P.) and one weaker as under proof (U.P.).`,`To convert: proof strength = percentage v/v x 100 / 57.1, that is roughly the percentage v/v multiplied by 1.753.`,`Example: 90 percent v/v alcohol has a proof strength of 90 x 1.753 = 157.7, so it is described as 57.7 over proof.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Remember 57.1 percent v/v`,text:`The single number most asked about proof spirit is that it contains about 57.1 percent v/v ethanol, equivalent to about 49.24 percent w/w. Everything else in this section follows from it.`}]},{topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced level is the compounding technique itself: the order of operations that gives an elegant, uniform and stable product, and the labels that must go on it.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Compounding a mixture`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Dissolve soluble solids in a part of the vehicle in the measuring cylinder or a beaker, using the smallest volume that works.`,`Add liquids in the order of their increasing viscosity and decreasing solubility in the vehicle.`,`Add any indiffusible solid as a smooth paste made with a suspending agent such as tragacanth or sodium carboxymethylcellulose, then dilute gradually.`,`Add resinous or alcoholic tinctures last, very slowly, with constant stirring, so that the resin precipitates in a fine dispersed form rather than as lumps.`,`Make up to the final volume with the vehicle, transfer to a bottle, label and check.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type of mixture`,`Example of the solid`,`Label needed`],rows:[[`Diffusible solid`,`Light kaolin, magnesium trisilicate`,`Shake well before use`],[`Indiffusible solid`,`Sulphur, calamine, chalk`,`Shake well before use, with a suspending agent`],[`Soluble solid`,`Potassium citrate, sodium salicylate`,`No suspending agent needed`],[`With resinous tincture`,`Tincture of benzoin, tincture of tolu`,`Add last, slowly, with stirring`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Lotions`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Lotions are applied without friction, unlike liniments, which are rubbed in. This single distinction is frequently asked.`,`A suspension lotion such as calamine lotion needs a suspending agent, commonly bentonite magma, and must carry the direction shake well before use.`,`Every lotion must be labelled for external use only, on a distinctive label, and must not be dispensed in the same style of container as an internal preparation.`,`The cooling effect of a lotion comes from evaporation of the vehicle; alcohol or glycerin content is adjusted to control this.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Powders`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Bulk powders for internal use are supplied in a wide-mouthed jar with a measuring spoon, and are limited to non-potent medicaments such as antacids.`,`Divided powders are weighed individually and wrapped in papers, so a potent drug can be given accurately.`,`Dusting powders are for external use, must be impalpably fine and free from grittiness, and are passed through a fine sieve before packing into a sifter-top container. A dusting powder intended for an open wound must be sterile.`,`Effervescent powders and granules contain an acid, usually citric or tartaric, with sodium bicarbonate, and are dissolved in water before taking.`,`Insufflations are for blowing into a body cavity and snuffs for the nose.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Reduce every ingredient to the same fine particle size before mixing.`,`When the quantities differ widely, mix by geometric dilution: mix the smallest quantity with an equal quantity of the diluent, then keep doubling with fresh diluent until all is incorporated.`,`Add coloured or potent ingredients between layers of the diluent so that the uniformity of the mix can be judged by eye.`,`Handle volatile or eutectic components with an inert absorbent as described under incompatibilities.`,`Mix light and bulky powders last and with a light hand, to avoid loss as dust.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Geometric dilution`,text:`Whenever a question gives a very small quantity of a potent drug to be mixed into a large bulk of diluent, the required technique is geometric dilution. It is the standard answer for content uniformity of a dispensed powder.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-dp-prescription-1`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The symbol Rx written at the head of a prescription constitutes the:`,options:[`Inscription`,`Superscription`,`Subscription`,`Signatura`],answer:1,explanation:`Rx is the superscription, an abbreviation of the Latin recipe meaning take thou, and it introduces the order. The inscription is the part that follows it and names the actual ingredients and their quantities.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-dp-prescription-2`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The abbreviation b.d. on a prescription means:`,options:[`Twice a day`,`Three times a day`,`Every day`,`At bedtime`],answer:0,explanation:`b.d. stands for bis in die, twice a day. Three times a day is t.d.s. or t.i.d., every day is o.d. from omni die, and at bedtime is h.s. from hora somni.`},{id:`q-dp-prescription-3`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A prescription reads: Tab. Paracetamol 500 mg, one tablet p.r.n. The instruction p.r.n. means the tablet is to be taken:`,options:[`Immediately`,`Before food`,`When required`,`As much as desired`],answer:2,explanation:`p.r.n. is pro re nata, when required, so the patient takes it only when the symptom appears. Immediately is stat (statim) and as much as desired is ad libitum, which is a different instruction entirely and would be unsafe here.`},{id:`q-dp-prescription-4`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The directions addressed to the pharmacist, such as "make a mixture, send 100 mL", form the:`,options:[`Signatura`,`Superscription`,`Inscription`,`Subscription`],answer:3,explanation:`The subscription is the prescriber talking to the pharmacist about how to prepare and how much to supply. The signatura is the part that talks to the patient and becomes the wording on the label.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-dp-prescription-5`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a prescription the abbreviation aa placed against two ingredients means:`,options:[`Up to, to make`,`Of each`,`A sufficient quantity`,`Of the same kind`],answer:1,explanation:`aa is from the Greek and Latin ana, meaning of each, so the stated quantity applies to every ingredient it follows. Up to is ad, and a sufficient quantity is q.s. (quantum sufficit), which is used for the vehicle, not for actives.`},{id:`q-dp-prescription-6`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, a prescription for a drug listed in Schedule X must be:`,options:[`Written in duplicate, with one copy retained by the pharmacist`,`Written in triplicate and sent to the licensing authority`,`Marked with a red border by the prescriber`,`Renewable at the discretion of the pharmacist`],answer:0,explanation:`Schedule X psychotropic substances require a prescription in duplicate; the pharmacist retains one copy for the period prescribed by the Rules and stocks are kept under lock and key. Schedule H1 supplies, by contrast, are recorded in a separate register rather than by retaining a duplicate prescription, and no schedule permits the pharmacist to renew such a prescription independently.`},{id:`q-dp-posology-1`,topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Young's rule for calculating a paediatric dose uses:`,options:[`Body weight in pounds`,`Age in years`,`Age in months`,`Body surface area`],answer:1,explanation:`Young's rule is child dose = adult dose x age/(age + 12), so it uses age in years. Age in months belongs to Fried's rule and weight in pounds to Clark's rule.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-dp-posology-2`,topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A large first dose given to reach the therapeutic concentration quickly is called the:`,options:[`Maintenance dose`,`Prophylactic dose`,`Loading dose`,`Minimum effective dose`],answer:2,explanation:`The loading or priming dose fills the volume of distribution rapidly so that the target concentration is reached without waiting several half-lives. The maintenance dose is the smaller repeated dose that then simply replaces what is eliminated.`},{id:`q-dp-posology-3`,topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The adult dose of a drug is 500 mg. By Young's rule, the dose for a child of 4 years is:`,options:[`100 mg`,`125 mg`,`160 mg`,`200 mg`],answer:1,explanation:`Dose = 500 x 4/(4 + 12) = 500 x 4/16 = 500 x 0.25 = 125 mg. The answer 100 mg comes from applying Dilling rule (age/20) instead, which uses a different denominator and is not what was asked.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-dp-posology-4`,topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The adult dose of a drug is 250 mg. By Clark's rule, the dose for a child weighing 50 pounds is closest to:`,options:[`50 mg`,`62.5 mg`,`83 mg`,`125 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`Clark's rule gives dose = 250 x 50/150 = 250/3 = 83.3 mg, about 83 mg. Using 200 pounds or 100 pounds as the adult weight gives the other options; the constant in Clark's rule is 150 pounds.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-dp-posology-5`,topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which factor does NOT normally require a reduction in the usual adult dose?`,options:[`Severe renal impairment`,`Advanced age`,`Hepatic cirrhosis`,`Giving the same total daily oral dose split into three divided doses instead of one`],answer:3,explanation:`Splitting the same total daily amount into divided doses changes the pattern of plasma concentration — lower peaks and higher troughs — but not the total quantity given, so no reduction in the daily dose is implied. Renal impairment, hepatic cirrhosis and advanced age all reduce clearance and therefore call for a smaller dose or a longer interval.`},{id:`q-dp-posology-6`,topicId:`dp-posology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A child has a body surface area of 0.6 m2. If the adult dose is 900 mg, the dose for the child by the body surface area rule is closest to:`,options:[`260 mg`,`312 mg`,`450 mg`,`540 mg`],answer:1,explanation:`Dose = adult dose x BSA/1.73 = 900 x 0.6/1.73 = 540/1.73 = 312 mg. Choosing 540 mg is the error of multiplying by 0.6 and forgetting to divide by the average adult surface area of 1.73 m2.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-dp-incompatibility-1`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Trituration of camphor with menthol in a mortar produces a damp liquid mass. This is an example of:`,options:[`Chemical incompatibility due to oxidation`,`Therapeutic incompatibility`,`Physical incompatibility due to eutexia`,`Adjusted incompatibility`],answer:2,explanation:`The two solids form a eutectic mixture whose melting point is below room temperature, so the mass liquefies; no new chemical substance is formed, which makes it a physical incompatibility. Nothing has been oxidised and the therapeutic action of both is unchanged.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-dp-incompatibility-2`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is an absorbent used to deal with a eutectic mixture in a powder?`,options:[`Light magnesium oxide`,`Sodium bicarbonate`,`Citric acid`,`Liquid paraffin`],answer:0,explanation:`Light magnesium oxide, along with light magnesium carbonate, light kaolin and starch, has a large bulk and surface area and takes up the liquefied eutectic to give a dry, uniform powder. Sodium bicarbonate and citric acid would react with each other in the presence of moisture rather than absorb anything.`},{id:`q-dp-incompatibility-3`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A prescription orders tetracycline capsules together with an aluminium and magnesium antacid suspension. This is an example of:`,options:[`Physical incompatibility`,`Chemical incompatibility producing effervescence`,`Therapeutic incompatibility`,`Tolerated incompatibility needing no action`],answer:2,explanation:`Tetracycline chelates the divalent and trivalent metal ions, so absorption of the antibiotic falls and the patient is under-treated even though each product is pharmaceutically perfect. It is therefore a therapeutic incompatibility, and it is managed by separating the times of administration after consulting the prescriber.`},{id:`q-dp-incompatibility-4`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Addition of tincture of benzoin directly to a large volume of water in a mixture produces a coarse lumpy precipitate. The correct dispensing procedure is to:`,options:[`Warm the water before adding the tincture`,`Add the tincture slowly to the vehicle with constant stirring`,`Add the water slowly to the whole of the tincture`,`Replace the tincture with an equal volume of alcohol`],answer:1,explanation:`The resin is insoluble in water and must be thrown out in a finely dispersed form, which happens when the tincture is added last, in a thin stream, to the stirred vehicle. Adding water to the tincture reverses the dilution and gives exactly the coarse lumps you are trying to avoid, and substituting an ingredient would change the prescription.`},{id:`q-dp-incompatibility-5`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which pair would react with visible effervescence if dispensed together in aqueous solution?`,options:[`Silver nitrate and sodium chloride`,`Potassium permanganate and glycerin`,`Sodium bicarbonate and citric acid`,`An alkaloidal salt and sodium hydroxide`],answer:2,explanation:`Bicarbonate reacts with the acid to liberate carbon dioxide, which is seen as effervescence and can burst a tightly closed bottle. Silver nitrate with a chloride gives a white precipitate and an alkaloidal salt with alkali precipitates the free base, but neither produces a gas.`},{id:`q-dp-incompatibility-6`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`An incompatibility that can be overcome merely by changing the order of mixing or by diluting the solutions, without adding or substituting any ingredient, is described as:`,options:[`Adjusted incompatibility`,`Therapeutic incompatibility`,`Purposeful incompatibility`,`Tolerated incompatibility`],answer:3,explanation:`A tolerated incompatibility is corrected by technique alone, so the pharmacist may act without consulting the prescriber. An adjusted incompatibility requires an ingredient to be added, removed or replaced, and that change can only be made with the agreement of the prescriber.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-dp-dosage-forms-1`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A solution of ratio strength 1 in 1000 w/v corresponds to:`,options:[`1 percent w/v`,`0.1 percent w/v`,`0.01 percent w/v`,`10 percent w/v`],answer:1,explanation:`1 in 1000 means 1 g in 1000 mL, that is 0.1 g in 100 mL, which is 0.1 percent w/v. The value 0.01 percent w/v would correspond to a ratio strength of 1 in 10000.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-dp-dosage-forms-2`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Proof spirit contains approximately what percentage of ethanol by volume?`,options:[`42.9 percent`,`49.2 percent`,`57.1 percent`,`95.0 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`Proof spirit is about 57.1 percent v/v ethanol, which corresponds to about 49.24 percent w/w. The figure 49.2 is the weight in weight value, offered here as the standard distractor.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-dp-dosage-forms-3`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The quantity of sodium chloride required to prepare 500 mL of a 0.9 percent w/v solution is:`,options:[`0.45 g`,`4.5 g`,`9.0 g`,`45 g`],answer:1,explanation:`Quantity = 0.9 x 500/100 = 4.5 g. The value 9.0 g would be needed for a full litre, so it is the answer to a different question and is the commonest slip here.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-dp-dosage-forms-4`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`To prepare 900 mL of 70 percent v/v alcohol from 95 percent and 50 percent alcohols by alligation, the volume of 95 percent alcohol needed is:`,options:[`500 mL`,`450 mL`,`400 mL`,`360 mL`],answer:2,explanation:`Parts of 95 percent = 70 - 50 = 20; parts of 50 percent = 95 - 70 = 25; total 45 parts. Volume of 95 percent = 20/45 x 900 = 400 mL, and the remaining 500 mL is the 50 percent alcohol. Taking 25 parts for the strong alcohol reverses the diagonal subtraction and gives the distractor 500 mL.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-dp-dosage-forms-5`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A very small quantity of a potent drug has to be mixed uniformly into a large bulk of lactose. The correct technique is:`,options:[`Trituration of the whole quantity at once in a mortar`,`Geometric dilution`,`Levigation with a small volume of liquid`,`Sifting the two powders together through a coarse sieve`],answer:1,explanation:`In geometric dilution the potent drug is first mixed with an equal quantity of diluent, and the mix is then doubled repeatedly with fresh diluent until all of it is used, which guarantees uniform distribution. Levigation is the technique for reducing particle size of a solid in a liquid for ointments and suspensions, not for blending powders.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-dp-dosage-forms-6`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`What volume of water must be added to 250 mL of 90 percent v/v alcohol to reduce its strength to 30 percent v/v?`,options:[`250 mL`,`500 mL`,`600 mL`,`750 mL`],answer:1,explanation:`The absolute alcohol present is 250 x 90/100 = 225 mL, and to be 30 percent this must occupy a final volume of 225 x 100/30 = 750 mL, so 750 - 250 = 500 mL of water is added. Choosing 750 mL is the error of reporting the final volume instead of the volume of water added.`,tag:`Numerical`}],n=[{id:`f-dp-prescription-1`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,front:`Parts of a prescription in order?`,back:`Date, patient details, superscription (Rx), inscription, subscription, signatura, refill, prescriber signature.`},{id:`f-dp-prescription-2`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,front:`Subscription versus signatura?`,back:`Subscription = directions to the pharmacist. Signatura = directions to the patient, which go on the label.`},{id:`f-dp-prescription-3`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,front:`b.d., t.d.s., q.i.d.?`,back:`Twice a day, three times a day, four times a day.`},{id:`f-dp-prescription-4`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,front:`a.c., p.c., h.s., stat, p.r.n.?`,back:`Before food, after food, at bedtime, immediately, when required.`},{id:`f-dp-prescription-5`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,front:`q.s., aa, ad, gtt, m.d.u.?`,back:`A sufficient quantity, of each, up to, drops, to be used as directed.`},{id:`f-dp-prescription-6`,topicId:`dp-prescription`,front:`Four parts of the inscription?`,back:`Base (active), adjuvant, corrective and vehicle.`},{id:`f-dp-posology-1`,topicId:`dp-posology`,front:`Young's rule?`,back:`Child dose = adult dose x age in years / (age + 12).`},{id:`f-dp-posology-2`,topicId:`dp-posology`,front:`Clark's rule?`,back:`Child dose = adult dose x weight in pounds / 150 (or weight in kg / 70).`},{id:`f-dp-posology-3`,topicId:`dp-posology`,front:`Dilling's and Fried's rules?`,back:`Dilling: adult dose x age in years / 20. Fried: adult dose x age in months / 150.`},{id:`f-dp-posology-4`,topicId:`dp-posology`,front:`Body surface area dose rule?`,back:`Child dose = adult dose x BSA in m2 / 1.73; the most accurate method.`},{id:`f-dp-posology-5`,topicId:`dp-posology`,front:`Cockcroft-Gault equation?`,back:`CrCl = (140 - age) x weight in kg / (72 x serum creatinine in mg/dL); x 0.85 for females.`},{id:`f-dp-posology-6`,topicId:`dp-posology`,front:`Guiding principle for geriatric dosing?`,back:`Start low and go slow; renal and hepatic clearance both fall with age.`},{id:`f-dp-incompatibility-1`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,front:`Three classes of incompatibility?`,back:`Physical (pharmaceutical), chemical and therapeutic.`},{id:`f-dp-incompatibility-2`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,front:`What is a eutectic mixture?`,back:`Two solids whose mixture melts below room temperature, so they liquefy on trituration, for example camphor with menthol or thymol.`},{id:`f-dp-incompatibility-3`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,front:`How is a eutectic powder dispensed?`,back:`Mix lightly without pressure, or absorb the liquid on light magnesium oxide, light kaolin or starch; or dispense the parts separately.`},{id:`f-dp-incompatibility-4`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,front:`Tolerated versus adjusted incompatibility?`,back:`Tolerated: cured by technique alone. Adjusted: an ingredient must be added or changed, so the prescriber must agree.`},{id:`f-dp-incompatibility-5`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,front:`Example of therapeutic incompatibility?`,back:`Tetracycline with an antacid (chelation), or a sulphonamide with procaine (PABA antagonism).`},{id:`f-dp-incompatibility-6`,topicId:`dp-incompatibility`,front:`Who may correct a therapeutic incompatibility?`,back:`Only the prescriber; the pharmacist reports it and records the advice given.`},{id:`f-dp-dosage-forms-1`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,front:`Define percentage w/v, w/w and v/v.`,back:`w/v = g in 100 mL of product; w/w = g in 100 g of product; v/v = mL in 100 mL of product.`},{id:`f-dp-dosage-forms-2`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,front:`Alligation alternate in one line?`,back:`Subtract diagonally: parts of strong = wanted minus weak; parts of weak = strong minus wanted.`},{id:`f-dp-dosage-forms-3`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,front:`Strength of proof spirit?`,back:`About 57.1 percent v/v ethanol, about 49.24 percent w/w. Stronger is over proof, weaker is under proof.`},{id:`f-dp-dosage-forms-4`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,front:`Lotion versus liniment?`,back:`A lotion is applied to the skin without friction; a liniment is rubbed in.`},{id:`f-dp-dosage-forms-5`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,front:`Order of adding ingredients to a mixture?`,back:`Soluble solids first in part of the vehicle, then liquids by increasing viscosity, suspended solids as a paste, resinous tinctures last with stirring.`},{id:`f-dp-dosage-forms-6`,topicId:`dp-dosage-forms`,front:`What is geometric dilution?`,back:`Mix the potent drug with an equal quantity of diluent, then keep doubling with fresh diluent until all is incorporated.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};